"""Original detailed teaching for Combination (printed pp. 165–210).

Book diagrams are identified explicitly. Each displayed variation is explained
ply by ply; illustrative choices are distinguished from forced replies.
"""
from core.primer_build import after
from core.primer_chapter5 import P
from core.primer_guides_chapter1 import check, example, guide, section

GUIDES = {
'ch5-anatomy': guide((165,166),
    ('Separate the motif, idea, preparation and payoff.', 'Count the complete exchange before judging a sacrifice.'),
    (
        section('What makes a combination?', '''A combination is a coordinated sequence aimed at a concrete benefit. In the book’s terminology, it normally includes a material sacrifice and forcing play. The benefit might be mate, material, a draw, or a better position. A sacrifice is therefore a means, not the goal. Giving away a rook without a calculated return is simply losing a rook. At this level, begin with short combinations whose final result you can explain confidently. Learn to say what changes on the board, rather than remembering only a striking first move.'''),
        section('Name the motif and the idea', '''The motif is the feature that makes a tactic possible: here Black’s king on d8 and rook on g8 share a rank, while White’s knight on d5 can jump to e7. The idea is what you do with that feature: attract the king to c8, a square attacked by Ne7. White’s rook begins on a6, Black’s bishop on d7, and White’s king and pawn stand on e5 and f5. This inventory matters because the knight’s fork works only if its target squares and escape routes are exactly right.'''),
        section('Preparation, climax, payoff', '''Ra8+ starts with a skewer: the king stands between rook and target. The replay follows Black’s bishop block, Bc8. That block is a defensive choice, not the only legal answer to the first check. Now Rxc8+ is the climax. White deliberately offers a rook for a bishop because Kxc8 exposes king and rook to Ne7+. The king must answer the knight check, after which Nxg8 collects the rook. White has traded one rook for a bishop and rook, leaving a knight and pawn against a bare king.'''),
        section('Your calculation checkpoint', '''Before advancing the replay, cover the next move and name both fork targets. Then calculate from the defender’s side: can the king refuse the sacrifice, capture the knight, or move while protecting the rook? At each branch, update the pieces removed and the side to move. In your own games, inspect checks and captures first, then ask whether a forcing move can put a target onto a tactical square. This example demonstrates one defensive branch. You must still inspect other replies to Ra8+ before claiming a complete forced line.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 265: attraction after a blocking defence',P['ANATOMY'],('Ra8+','Bc8','Rxc8+','Kxc8','Ne7+','Kd7','Nxg8'),(
        'White: king e5, rook a6, knight d5, pawn f5. Black: king d8, rook g8, bishop d7. Find the fork square before playing.',
        'Ra8+ attacks the king along the eighth rank, with the g8 rook behind it.',
        'Black chooses a bishop block. Other legal king replies require separate analysis; this line follows the book’s main defensive choice.',
        'Rxc8+ removes the blocker and offers the rook to the king. The knight remains ready on d5.',
        'The king accepts and arrives on c8, now a knight jump away from e7.',
        'Ne7+ attacks c8 and g8. The checking tempo prevents Black from simply saving the rook.',
        'Kd7 is an illustrative legal king reply; it cannot save the g8 rook.',
        'Nxg8 completes the payoff. Black has lost rook and bishop; White has lost only the rook.',
    )),),
    ('Do not stop counting when your own rook disappears.', 'A main line is not a proof that every earlier reply is forced.'),
    (check('ch5-anatomy-motif','Which feature is the motif?',('The king and rook can become targets of Ne7+',True,'Their geometry makes the knight fork possible.'),('The rook sacrifice itself',False,'That is the chosen tactical action, not the underlying feature.')),
     check('ch5-anatomy-count','What does White exchange away in the main line?',('A rook for Black’s bishop and rook',True,'The final fork recovers the rook after the bishop has already been captured.'),('A rook for only a bishop',False,'That stops the calculation before Ne7+ and Nxg8.'))),
    ('Identify the feature that permits a tactic.','Connect preparation, sacrifice and payoff.','Count at a stable endpoint.')),

'ch5-queen-fork': guide((166,167),
    ('Recognize a capture that also defends a piece.', 'Test queen offers by calculating the checking fork.'),
    (
        section('A loose knight changes the calculation', '''White has king b4, queen e4, knight g5 and pawns a4 and h5. Black has king d8, queen f6, knight e5 and pawn b7. Black’s queen attacks g5, so a casual queen move could leave White’s knight hanging. Look for a move that performs two jobs. Qxe5 captures the enemy knight and also defends g5 along the fifth rank. This second function explains why the apparent queen offer works. A tactic is often hidden because we notice a move’s attack but overlook its defence.'''),
        section('See the square beyond the sacrifice', '''After Qxe5, Black can capture the white queen with Qxe5. Imagine the resulting board before moving: Black’s queen stands on e5 and its king remains on d8. A white knight on f7 attacks both squares. Nf7+ therefore forces the king to move before the queen can escape. This is attraction of a queen onto a fork square. The knight does not have to capture a piece immediately; its checking double attack earns the decisive tempo for the following capture.'''),
        section('Count the exchanges and test refusal', '''The replay shows acceptance of the queen offer, followed by a sample king retreat and Nxe5. Both queens have disappeared, along with Black’s knight; White’s knight survives. White has gained a minor piece. But Black is not obliged to take the queen. If the queen moves elsewhere, White has already captured e5 and has defended g5. Check that claim on the actual board rather than assuming every queen offer must be accepted. The value of the tactic comes from the favorable alternatives, not from hoping that Black enjoys taking queens.'''),
        section('Use this pattern in ordinary games', '''Before revealing Nf7+, list the knight’s eight possible jumps and identify which one checks. When a move attacks your queen or a loose piece, consider a forcing counterattack, a capture with defence, and a safe retreat. Compare them without panic. Queen offers are easy to reject automatically because the material seems enormous; they are also easy to overvalue after learning a beautiful example. The cure for both errors is the same: write down the opponent’s best capture, inspect checks, and account for every piece at the end.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 267: capture, defence, attraction and fork',P['QUEEN_FORK'],('Qxe5','Qxe5','Nf7+','Ke7','Nxe5'),(
        'White’s g5 knight is attacked by the f6 queen. Find a queen capture that will also protect that knight.',
        'Qxe5 takes the black knight and protects g5 horizontally. This quiet capture does not give check.',
        'Black accepts the queen offer. The queen on e5 and king on d8 are now a knight fork apart.',
        'Nf7+ checks d8 and attacks e5. Black must answer the check.',
        'Ke7 is a sample legal king escape. Other king escapes likewise leave the queen attacked.',
        'Nxe5 recovers the queen. With queens exchanged, White retains the extra knight.',
    )),),
    ('The queen capture is an option for Black, not an obligation.','Do not miss that Qxe5 protects the g5 knight.'),
    (check('ch5-qfork-defence','Why does Qxe5 also help the knight on g5?',('The queen defends it along the fifth rank',True,'The path e5–f5–g5 is clear.'),('It checks the black king',False,'Qxe5 is not check in this position.')),
     check('ch5-qfork-tempo','Why does Black not save the queen after Nf7+?',('The king is in check and must be made safe first',True,'The check is what makes the second attack decisive.'),('A queen is forbidden to move when attacked',False,'An attacked queen may move unless a more urgent legal obligation prevents it.'))),
    ('Look for moves with both attacking and defensive jobs.','Attract a target onto a fork square.','Inspect acceptance and refusal.')),

'ch5-promotion-fork': guide((167,167),
    ('Choose a promotion by its immediate effect.', 'Connect two attractions to a checking knight fork.'),
    (
        section('Inventory a complicated position', '''White has king f5, queen b6, rook c5 and pawns b7 and g6. Black has king b8, queen e7, rook h8 and pawns f6 and g7. White’s b-pawn is close to promotion, but the enemy rook and queen make a slow plan dangerous. The key targets are the black king and queen. A knight on c8 attacks a7 and e7. One target already occupies the right square; the other must be drawn onto a7. This is the goal around which the moves are organized.'''),
        section('Put a capturable piece on the promotion square', '''Rc8+ invites the black rook from h8 to capture on c8. In the shown branch, that capture places an enemy piece diagonally in front of b7, so the pawn can promote with a capture. The king is still on b8, where a c8 knight would not check it. White therefore inserts Qa7+, offering the queen. After Kxa7 the required geometry is complete. Notice the order: the rook is placed on c8 before the king is attracted to a7. Do not jump to a promotion while its tactical conditions are missing.'''),
        section('Why a knight is worth more than a queen here', '''After the two sacrifices, bxc8=N+ checks the king on a7 and attacks the queen on e7. The king must move, then Nxe7 wins the queen. A new queen on c8 would be stronger in general but would not check a7. Black would have a free tempo, so the advertised fork disappears. This is not a rule to prefer knights whenever you promote with check. Compare every relevant promotion by checks, captures, escape squares and the opponent’s immediate reply. Piece value depends on the job required now.'''),
        section('Keep the accounting honest', '''Pause before the promotion and state where both queens and both rooks have gone. In this main line White gives a rook and queen, captures Black’s rook and queen, and turns the b-pawn into a surviving knight. The gain comes from the promotion and the tempo of check. Reset and try queen promotion to see the difference in legal replies. The guide follows the book’s principal capture branch; it does not label each earlier capture the only legal move. In a real game, inspect king retreats and other refusals as part of your calculation.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 269: two attractions and underpromotion',P['PROMOTE'],('Rc8+','Rxc8','Qa7+','Kxa7','bxc8=N+','Kb7','Nxe7'),(
        'White’s b7 pawn can capture on c8 if an enemy piece is placed there. A c8 knight would attack a7 and e7.',
        'Rc8+ offers the rook on the intended promotion square.',
        'Black accepts with the h8 rook. This is the main capture branch; it leaves a black rook available to the pawn.',
        'Qa7+ now offers the queen to place the king on the other knight-fork target.',
        'Kxa7 accepts. Before advancing, compare the checks produced by a queen and by a knight on c8.',
        'bxc8=N+ removes the rook and forks king a7 and queen e7. The knight’s movement is essential.',
        'Kb7 is a legal illustrative king retreat. The queen cannot be saved during this checking tempo.',
        'Nxe7 captures the queen and leaves White with a knight. Count both earlier sacrifices in the balance.',
    )),),
    ('Automatic queen promotion loses the checking tempo in this pattern.','Count the promoted pawn as a new piece only after its legal promotion.'),
    (check('ch5-promo-choice','Why is bxc8=N+ the demonstrated choice?',('It checks a7 and attacks e7',True,'The same knight jump geometry hits both targets.'),('Knights are always stronger than queens near the edge',False,'Only this exact tactical job makes the knight promotion preferable.')),
     check('ch5-promo-order','What does Qa7+ achieve after Rxc8?',('It attracts the king onto a square the promoted knight will attack',True,'The king must be on a7 for the c8 knight to check it.'),('It protects White’s king on f5',False,'Its essential role is attraction, not direct protection of f5.'))),
    ('Choose the required movement power.','Prepare both fork targets.','Value a forcing tempo concretely.')),

'ch5-open-lines': guide((168,168),
    ('Identify the exact blocked line in a mating net.', 'Verify king escapes before sacrificing to open it.'),
    (
        section('A line exists only when its path is clear', '''A bishop or rook may point toward the enemy king without actually attacking it because a piece blocks the path. In the first example White has king g6, queen f4 and bishop e7; Black has king h8, bishop g8 and pawns f5, g7 and h5. The bishop would like to reach f6 and check along f6–g7–h8. The pawn on g7 prevents that. The problem is therefore specific: force g7 to move while keeping the king confined. Simply adding an attacker is less efficient than removing the obstacle.'''),
        section('Calculate both replies to the queen check', '''Qh6+ checks next to the king. Black’s own bishop occupies g8, g7 is occupied by its pawn, and the white king controls h7 and f7. The king cannot capture the queen on h6 from h8. Black has two legal replies. The capture gxh6 moves the blocker off the diagonal, enabling Bf6 mate. Alternatively, Bh7+ blocks the queen’s check while checking White’s king. White answers Qxh7 mate, capturing that bishop. Before sacrificing, verify every answer to check, including counterchecks. Both branches win here, but they use different finishing moves.'''),
        section('A pawn can deliver the finishing blow', '''The second example uses the same idea with different machinery. White has king f5, queen h6, bishop e5 and pawn f6; Black has king h8, rook f7 and pawn h7. Qf8+ draws the rook from f7 to f8. Now f7 both advances the pawn and opens the bishop’s e5–f6–g7–h8 diagonal. The resulting check is delivered by the bishop, even though the last move is a pawn move. The pawn also blocks the rook’s return down the f-file. Identify every changed line after a move.'''),
        section('Transfer and limits', '''Pause before each final move and enumerate the king’s safe squares, captures of the checker and possible blocks. Then reset and ask how the defender could have avoided this net earlier: making luft, exchanging an attacker, keeping control of the entry square, or avoiding the original confinement. In your games, a sacrifice to open a file is justified by the action available on that file afterward. An open line without an effective entry or a mating threat is not compensation by itself. The small examples isolate the mechanism; fuller positions add defensive resources.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 272: force open a bishop diagonal',P['LINES'],('Qh6+','gxh6','Bf6#'),(
        'The g7 pawn blocks f6–g7–h8. White’s king helps confine Black. Calculate every reply to Qh6+ first.',
        'Qh6+ gives check down the h-file. The queen is offered to the g-pawn.',
        'gxh6 is one of two legal answers. It empties g7; the alternative Bh7+ is shown separately.',
        'Bf6# uses the newly cleared diagonal. Black’s own g8 bishop and the white king remove the escapes.',
    )),example('Diagram 273: pawn move opens the diagonal',P['PAWN_MATE'],('Qf8+','Rxf8','f7#'),(
        'The f6 pawn blocks White’s e5 bishop. The rook on f7 must be drawn away so the pawn can move.',
        'Qf8+ offers the queen while checking the trapped king on h8.',
        'Rxf8 is forced, clearing f7 for the pawn but placing the rook behind its future blocker.',
        'f7# opens the bishop’s diagonal to h8. The pawn move gives discovered checkmate.',
    )),example('Diagram 272: answer the bishop countercheck',P['LINES'],('Qh6+','Bh7+','Qxh7#'),(
        'Calculate both defences to the queen check: a pawn capture and a bishop block.',
        'Qh6+ offers the queen, but Black is not obliged to capture it.',
        'Bh7+ blocks the h-file and checks White’s king on g6 along the diagonal. White must answer that check.',
        'Qxh7# removes the checking bishop with mate. White’s king on g6 protects the queen on h7.',
    ))),
    ('Opening a line is useful only if your pieces can exploit it.','The piece just moved need not be the piece giving check.'),
    (check('ch5-lines-blocker','Why does gxh6 help White?',('It clears g7 from the future bishop diagonal',True,'The bishop can then attack h8 through g7.'),('It moves Black’s king away from the corner',False,'The king remains on h8 throughout.')),
     check('ch5-lines-pawn','After f7 in the second example, which piece checks the king?',('The bishop on e5',True,'The pawn has uncovered its diagonal.'),('Only the pawn on f7',False,'That pawn attacks e8 and g8, not the king on h8.'))),
    ('Name the blocked line.','Force its blocker away.','Verify the complete mating net.')),

'ch5-obstruction': guide((167,168),
    ('Distinguish opening your line from blocking the opponent’s escape.', 'Use a forced capture to obstruct a square.'),
    (
        section('The enemy army can imprison its own king', '''White has king c1, rook d2 and knight b4. Black has king a1, bishop c4 and pawn a3. Black’s king has very little space, but a2 is not initially occupied. A knight on c2 would check a1; it would not by itself control a2. That escape must be dealt with before the knight moves. This is an obstruction problem: arrange for an enemy piece to occupy the square the king needs. The sacrificed piece’s destination matters more than the number of points it costs.'''),
        section('Force the obstruction into place', '''Ra2+ places the rook beside the king. The black king cannot take it because White’s b4 knight protects a2. It cannot step to b1 or b2 because White’s c1 king controls those squares. The bishop on c4 can capture down the diagonal c4–b3–a2, and Bxa2 is the only legal reply. Black has won a rook, but its bishop now occupies the only relevant escape square. White follows with Nc2 mate. A knight check cannot be blocked, which makes the final move particularly effective against this boxed king.'''),
        section('Read the final position from the defender’s side', '''After Nc2, the knight checks a1. Black cannot capture that knight with the king, bishop or pawn. The king cannot move to a2 because its bishop is there, or to b1 or b2 because White’s king controls both. The pawn cannot answer the check, even though it may have an ordinary move in another position. This last distinction is useful: a side in check needs a move that removes the check, not merely any legal-looking move by any piece. Count actual check evasions, not imaginary turns.'''),
        section('Look for obstruction in larger positions', '''The same motif occurs when a sacrificed queen makes a rook block a back rank, a pawn capture seals the king’s flight square, or an enemy piece cuts contact between its defenders. Before moving the rook in the exercise, state why Kxa2 is illegal; before moving the knight, name every king escape. At your board, examine the effect of an enemy capture on its own army. Captures change both material and geometry. Do not assume the pattern works when your supporting knight or king is one square farther away.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 271: a rook buys an occupied escape square',P['OBSTRUCTION'],('Ra2+','Bxa2','Nc2#'),(
        'Black’s a1 king is confined by White’s c1 king and b4 knight. The a2 square needs to be occupied before Nc2.',
        'Ra2+ offers the rook. Kxa2 is illegal because the b4 knight protects it.',
        'Bxa2 is forced. Black’s own bishop now occupies the king’s potential escape square.',
        'Nc2# checks a1 by a knight jump. The bishop blocks a2 and the white king controls b1 and b2.',
    )),),
    ('The black king cannot capture a protected rook just because it is nearby.','Checkmate depends on all escapes, not only the checking knight.'),
    (check('ch5-obstruction-capture','Why can Black not play Kxa2?',('The b4 knight attacks a2',True,'A king may not move into an enemy attack.'),('Kings may never capture rooks',False,'A king can capture an unprotected rook when the destination is safe.')),
     check('ch5-obstruction-final','What job does Black’s bishop do after Bxa2?',('It obstructs its own king’s escape',True,'Its occupied square becomes part of White’s mating net.'),('It blocks the knight’s checking line',False,'A knight has no line that a bishop can block.'))),
    ('Use captures to alter escape squares.','Prove the capture is forced.','Verify a knight mate square by square.')),

'ch5-promotion-deflection': guide((169,169),
    ('Recognize a rook’s defensive job behind a pawn.', 'Compare accepting and declining a checking sacrifice.'),
    (
        section('Identify what prevents the obvious move', '''White has king d4, rook b4 and pawn a7. Black has king d6 and rook a6. The pawn appears ready to become a queen, but a8=Q is met by Rxa8: the defending rook travels through the square the pawn just vacated. Counting the pawn’s distance from promotion is not enough; inspect the promotion square after the pawn moves. The black rook’s essential job is control of the a-file. If it is removed or diverted, White can obtain the stronger promoted piece.'''),
        section('Use check to combine two threats', '''Rb6+ attacks the king across the sixth rank. If Black accepts with Rxb6, the rook abandons the a-file and a8=Q succeeds. If Black moves the king instead, White can normally take the rook with Rxa6 and keep the advanced pawn. Thus White is not relying on the defender voluntarily helping promotion. The check creates a choice between losing the a-file defender outright and diverting it. Analyze the legal king moves as well as the capture; a sacrifice is sound because of its alternatives, not because its main line is attractive.'''),
        section('The endpoint is an ending, not a checkmate', '''After Rxb6 and a8=Q, White has queen and king against rook and king. That is a major improvement and a normally winning material advantage, but the game has not ended. The new queen gives no check in this exact position. White must avoid losing it to a skewer or allowing stalemate later. The lesson’s claim concerns the successful transformation and material advantage, not an immediate mate. Learning to separate winning a favorable ending from already having won the game prevents careless moves after a successful tactic.'''),
        section('A practical promotion checklist', '''Pause before Rb6+ and compare it with immediate promotion. Trace the defender’s path in each resulting position. During your games, ask which piece controls the promotion square, whether a checking move can displace it, and whether the enemy king or another piece can take over its job. Also look for an intermediate check after promotion: a new queen does not suspend the opponent’s turn. This short diagram teaches one deflection, while more complicated pawn races may require rook offers, interference at crossing lines, or underpromotion to gain a crucial tempo.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 274: divert the rook with check',P['DEFLECTION'],('Rb6+','Rxb6','a8=Q'),(
        'White’s a7 pawn cannot safely queen while the a6 rook controls a8. Identify a forcing diversion.',
        'Rb6+ attacks the king and offers the rook to the a6 defender.',
        'Black accepts. The rook reaches b6 and loses its control over a8.',
        'a8=Q succeeds. White now has queen versus rook; this is an advantageous ending, not immediate mate.',
    )),example('A king retreat leaves the defender capturable',P['DEFLECTION'],('Rb6+','Kd7','Rxa6'),(
        'Calculate a refusal before assuming Black must capture your rook.',
        'The same rook check asks Black to choose a defence.',
        'Kd7 declines the offered rook, but the a6 rook remains attacked.',
        'Rxa6 removes the defender entirely. White keeps rook and advanced pawn against a king.',
    ))),
    ('Immediate promotion permits Rxa8.','Queen versus rook still requires careful conversion.'),
    (check('ch5-deflect-task','What job does the a6 rook perform?',('It controls a8 behind the pawn',True,'When the pawn leaves a7, the rook’s path to a8 opens.'),('It prevents the pawn from legally moving',False,'Promotion is legal, but the promoted piece would be captured.')),
     check('ch5-deflect-end','After Rxb6 a8=Q, what has White achieved?',('A favorable queen-versus-rook ending',True,'There is a large material advantage, but no checkmate yet.'),('The game has ended automatically',False,'Promotion is not itself a game-ending event.'))),
    ('Find the defender’s exact job.','Inspect capture and refusal.','Convert the resulting ending carefully.')),

'ch5-fortress-rescue': guide((169,169),
    ('Use exchanges to aim for a known drawing mechanism.', 'Recognize the wrong bishop and rook-pawn corner.'),
    (
        section('Defence can have a positive tactical goal', '''White has king b4, rook d4 and bishop c4. Black has king b8, rook c7, bishop c6 and pawns a7 and b7. Saving a draw can justify sacrifices just as winning material can. The target ending is a wrong-colored bishop with rook pawns: Black’s light-squared bishop cannot control the dark promotion corner a1. White wants to remove the rooks, convert the b-pawn into another a-pawn, and bring the king to a1. Every part matters; leaving a b-pawn or arriving too late could destroy the drawing mechanism.'''),
        section('Remove the pieces that can break the shelter', '''Rd8+ starts a rook exchange. The displayed defence is Rc8, followed by Rxc8+ Kxc8. White then plays Ba6, offering the bishop. If bxa6, Black has doubled a-pawns and the light-squared bishop. The b-pawn has lost the ability to advance down a neighboring file and drive the king away from the corner. The bishop sacrifice therefore changes the type of ending. White does not expect to regain the bishop: the compensation is a durable defensive arrangement that makes Black’s extra material insufficient.'''),
        section('The corner must actually be reachable', '''After bxa6, the replay shows White’s king traveling through a3 and a2 to a1 while Black makes illustrative king moves. This route is a demonstration of the shelter, not a claim that Black must choose those exact moves. Once White occupies a1, the bishop cannot check along that square’s color. When a black a-pawn arrives at a2, the white king may have no legal move and stalemate can result. Black’s king alone cannot force the white king out while obeying the rule that kings may not stand adjacent.'''),
        section('Test refusals and avoid overgeneralization', '''Black need not capture Ba6 immediately. The bishop’s placement still interferes with the useful pawn structure; the source also discusses delaying the capture until White’s bishop reaches b7. Before advancing the example, name the promotion square and its color, then compare that with the black bishop’s color. In your games, do not memorize “bishop and rook pawn draws.” A correctly colored bishop, a defender cut off from the corner, another pawn file, or another active piece changes the result. Recognize the exact shelter and calculate how to reach it.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 275: offer material to remove the useful pawn',P['FORTRESS'],('Rd8+','Rc8','Rxc8+','Kxc8','Ba6','bxa6','Ka3','Kc7','Ka2','Kd6','Ka1'),(
        'White aims for a1, which Black’s c6 bishop cannot control. First remove the rooks and the b-pawn’s separate file.',
        'Rd8+ forces Black to address the eighth-rank check.',
        'Rc8 is the source’s blocking defence, allowing the rooks to be exchanged.',
        'Rxc8+ removes Black’s rook, the piece that could attack either color.',
        'Kxc8 recaptures. White now has the opportunity to alter Black’s pawn structure.',
        'Ba6 offers the bishop to the b7 pawn. This is a drawing sacrifice, not an attempt to win material.',
        'bxa6 accepts, leaving black pawns on a7 and a6 and a bishop unable to control a1.',
        'Ka3 starts the safe route toward the corner. The following king moves illustrate arrival at the shelter.',
        'Kc7 is a legal illustrative approach by Black; it does not stop White’s route.',
        'Ka2 brings the defender one step from the required corner.',
        'Kd6 illustrates Black’s attempt to approach. The wrong bishop still cannot attack a1.',
        'Ka1 reaches the shelter. The drawing mechanism depends on staying in or returning to this corner.',
    )),),
    ('A bishop’s color is fixed; compare it with the pawn’s promotion square.','A familiar fortress is useful only if the king can reach it in time.'),
    (check('ch5-fortress-pawn','Why offer Ba6 to the b7 pawn?',('To leave Black with only a-file pawns',True,'The separate b-pawn is a resource the defender needs to eliminate.'),('To make Black’s bishop change color',False,'No pawn capture can change a bishop’s square color.')),
     check('ch5-fortress-corner','Which fact is essential to the shelter?',('Black’s bishop cannot control a1 and White can reach it',True,'Color and king access together explain the defence.'),('White is only a bishop down',False,'Material count alone does not prove a draw.'))),
    ('Choose a concrete defensive ending.','Remove the resources that defeat it.','Verify color and king access.')),

'ch5-stalemate-rescue': guide((169,170),
    ('Use king confinement as a defensive resource.', 'Check captures and refusals in a stalemate combination.'),
    (
        section('Reverse the usual material objective', '''White has king h5, rook c4, bishop d2 and pawn d5. Black has king d7, queen e7 and bishop f5. The white king has no safe move: the queen and bishop control the surrounding squares. Usually this is bad news, but it becomes a drawing resource if White can remove every other movable unit without being checked. Stalemate is a draw because the side to move has no legal move and its king is not attacked. The objective is therefore to lose the rook, pawn and bishop under favorable conditions.'''),
        section('Make the opponent’s captures serve your plan', '''Rc7+ offers the rook with check. After Kxc7, d6+ offers the pawn, again forcing Black to address its king. In the shown line Qxd6 captures that pawn. Now Bf4 attacks the queen along f4–e5–d6. The bishop is offered as the final mobile white piece. If Qxf4, White is left with a king that is not in check and cannot move: stalemate. Calculate the final king squares before making the first sacrifice. The tactic depends on precise coverage, not merely on being far behind in material.'''),
        section('What if the defender refuses?', '''Bf4 does not compel Qxf4 as the only legal move. Black can move the king or bishop and allow Bxd6, after which the queen disappears and the resulting small ending offers no winning material. The combination creates a defensive choice rather than a mandatory suicide capture. Earlier, Black can also capture the pawn with the king; White then uses a checking bishop move as the source explains. For a beginner, the main line makes the stalemate mechanism visible, but the presence of alternate defences must remain explicit.'''),
        section('Inspect every legal move, including pawns', '''Pause after each sacrifice and ask which white pieces still have moves. A forgotten pawn push would invalidate a planned stalemate even when the king is perfectly trapped. Likewise, a final checking capture would produce check rather than stalemate, and the king might then be checkmated. In your own losing games, inspect forcing checks, perpetual-check routes, a fortress, and stalemate before giving up. Those are resources to calculate, not excuses to ignore the position. From the stronger side, look for this exact danger when greedily capturing the opponent’s last mobile piece.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 276: three offers lead to stalemate',P['STALEMATE'],('Rc7+','Kxc7','d6+','Qxd6','Bf4','Qxf4'),(
        'White’s h5 king is already confined. Calculate how to remove rook, pawn and bishop without checking that king.',
        'Rc7+ offers the rook with check, making the sacrifice urgent.',
        'The king accepts. White now has bishop and pawn to dispose of.',
        'd6+ offers the pawn and checks the c7 king.',
        'The queen captures. The king capture is a separate defensive branch, not this replay.',
        'Bf4 attacks the queen and offers the bishop. Black can refuse, but then White can capture the queen.',
        'Qxf4 removes White’s last mobile piece. The white king is not in check and has no safe square: stalemate.',
    )),),
    ('An uncaptured spare pawn can ruin your stalemate plan.','The final capture must leave your king out of check.'),
    (check('ch5-stale-condition','Why is the last position a draw?',('White has no legal move and is not in check',True,'These are exactly the conditions for stalemate.'),('White has only a king',False,'A bare king can lose if it is checkmated.')),
     check('ch5-stale-refusal','Must Black capture Bf4?',('No; refusals must also be checked',True,'The queen attack supplies another defensive benefit if the sacrifice is declined.'),('Yes; attacked queens must capture',False,'There is no obligation to capture an attacking piece.'))),
    ('Count all remaining legal moves.','Remove mobile units with forcing play.','Inspect refusal as well as stalemate acceptance.')),

'ch5-quiet-move': guide((171,174),
    ('Distinguish a relative pin from a legal restriction.', 'Compare the urgency of threats before saving material.'),
    (
        section('Inventory the apparent problem', '''Black has king h8, queen e5, rook d7, bishops b7 and d4, and pawns a6, b5, g7 and h6. White has king h1, queen d3, rook d1, bishop b1, knight f5 and pawns a2, e4, g2 and h2. The bishop on d4 stands between the white rook and Black’s rook. Moving it seems to lose the d7 rook. This is a relative pin: moving the bishop is legal because Black’s king is not exposed. The question is whether a stronger threat compensates for the rook’s exposure.'''),
        section('A quiet move changes two lines', '''Bg1 does not check or capture. It opens the d-file so Black’s rook attacks White’s queen on d3, and it supports a mating attack against h2. After Qxh2, a king capture is forbidden because the g1 bishop protects h2. The bishop’s departure has therefore changed both attack and defence. Do not assess the move by staring at the now-exposed rook alone. Compare whose threat must be answered first. White has a queen under attack and a king threatened with mate; an ordinary material gain may be too slow.'''),
        section('Follow a plausible capture to its end', '''The replay shows White taking the newly exposed rook with Qxd7. This is a legal queen capture, not a rook capture: White’s own queen on d3 blocks the rook on d1. Black then plays Qxh2 mate. White’s king cannot take the queen, cannot move to g1 because the bishop occupies it, and cannot escape to g2 because its own pawn is there. Before advancing the final move, explain each of those facts. The replay demonstrates why this tempting capture fails; it does not enumerate every possible White defence.'''),
        section('Quiet does not mean unforcing', '''A move without check can be very forcing when it creates two urgent problems. The chapter also presents quiet moves that exploit pins, clear entry squares or threaten mate after a sacrifice. Searching only checks and captures would miss them. In your games, first establish the opponent’s immediate threat, then inspect forcing threats of your own before retreating a pinned piece. Verify the final mating net and the opponent’s strongest defence. Moving any pinned piece is not a general solution: an absolute pin to your king really does forbid exposing it.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 281: the rook capture overlooks mate', P['QUIET'], ('Bg1','Qxd7','Qxh2#'), (
        'Black’s d4 bishop is relatively pinned to the rook. Find a threat stronger than saving that rook.',
        'Bg1 clears the rook’s attack on d3 and supports Qxh2 mate. The bishop remains legally movable.',
        'White’s queen captures the rook. This illustrative mistake answers the material issue but ignores the king.',
        'Qxh2# ends the game: the g1 bishop protects the queen and Black’s net covers the king’s escapes.',
    )),),
    ('A relative pin does not make a move illegal.', 'Identify which piece can actually capture along a blocked file.'),
    (check('ch5-quiet-pin','Why can the d4 bishop move?',('Its departure does not expose Black’s king to check',True,'A pin to a rook creates a material risk rather than a legal prohibition.'),('Bishops can ignore every pin',False,'An absolute pin to the king can prevent a bishop from moving.')),
     check('ch5-quiet-queen','Why is Kxh2 impossible after Qxh2?',('The bishop on g1 protects h2',True,'The king cannot capture onto an attacked square.'),('A king can never capture a queen',False,'It may capture an unprotected queen if the destination is safe.'))),
    ('Check whether a pin is absolute.', 'Compare threats by urgency.', 'Inspect quiet moves with concrete threats.')),

'ch5-double-check': guide((194,195),
    ('Combine attraction, clearance and double check.', 'Explain why a double check requires a king move.'),
    (
        section('Development creates the opportunity', '''This position comes from Réti–Tartakower after the opening moves shown in the book. White’s king has castled to c1, bringing the rook from a1 to d1. The queen is on d3, bishops on d2 and f1, and knights on e4 and g1. Black’s king is still on e8; its queen is on e5 and its knight has just captured on e4. Most remaining pieces are undeveloped. The important inventory is the rook on the open d-file, the bishop on d2 blocking that rook’s view of d8, and the exposed central king.'''),
        section('Attract the king before clearing the line', '''Qd8+ puts the queen immediately beside the black king. Kxd8 is the only legal answer in this exact position. White has apparently given away its strongest piece, but the king now stands on the d-file behind White’s d2 bishop. Moving that bishop to g5 both checks along g5–f6–e7–d8 and uncovers the rook’s d-file check. Two pieces are checking at once. A queen or other piece cannot block both these different lines, and capturing just one checker leaves the other check intact.'''),
        section('Calculate the king’s destination', '''After Bg5+, Black must move its king. The replay follows Kc7, then Bd8 mate. The bishop’s new square checks c7 and remains protected by the rook on d1. The black king’s own army and White’s control eliminate its escapes. The other legal king reply to the double check is Ke8, when Rd8 is mate. Those two branches explain the combination without assuming that the source’s displayed king move is compulsory. Pause after the double check and identify both legal king destinations before choosing a branch.'''),
        section('Use the mechanism with restraint', '''This combination works because development has created immediate lines against a king that cannot find shelter. It is not a license to sacrifice a queen whenever a rook faces the center. Move the blocking bishop in your imagination first: does it give check itself, is the rook’s file genuinely clear, and where can the king go? Then inspect the mating move from the defender’s side. In your own opening games, develop pieces and castle so that tactical opportunities have support. When defending, notice a concealed rook behind a bishop before accepting a queen placed on that file.'''),
    ),
    (example('Réti–Tartakower: queen attraction and double check',P['RETI'],('Qd8+','Kxd8','Bg5+','Kc7','Bd8#'),(
        'White’s d1 rook points at d8 but its own bishop on d2 blocks the file. Black’s king is exposed on e8.',
        'Qd8+ attracts the king onto the rook’s file with a forcing queen offer.',
        'Kxd8 is forced. The king is now behind the d2 bishop on the d-file.',
        'Bg5+ checks with bishop and rook simultaneously. Count the two checking lines before continuing.',
        'Kc7 is one of two legal king replies. Ke8 instead permits Rd8 mate.',
        'Bd8# checks c7; the d1 rook protects the bishop, and there is no legal escape or capture.',
    )),example('The alternate king retreat',after(('Qd8+','Kxd8','Bg5+'),P['RETI']),('Ke8','Rd8#'),(
        'Black is in double check and can choose c7 or e8.',
        'Ke8 returns toward the starting square, but the d-file is clear.',
        'Rd8# uses that open file and the bishop’s protection of d8.',
    ))),
    ('The rook’s line must be completely clear after the bishop moves.', 'Do not assume a displayed king retreat is the only one.'),
    (check('ch5-double-response','How can Black answer Bg5+?',('By moving its king',True,'Capturing or blocking one checker leaves the other checking line.'),('By blocking only the bishop’s diagonal',False,'The rook still checks along the d-file.')),
     check('ch5-double-order','What does Qd8+ prepare?',('It places the king on the file that the bishop will uncover',True,'Attraction comes before the double check.'),('It wins Black’s queen immediately',False,'The purpose is a mating attack against the king.'))),
    ('Identify concealed lines.', 'Attract the king to their intersection.', 'Calculate every king reply.')),

'ch5-interference': guide((181,182),
    ('Find the crossing of two defensive lines.', 'Calculate both captures of an interference sacrifice.'),
    (
        section('Two pawns face two separate defenders', '''Black has king f5, bishops b4 and e4, and passed pawns d3 and h3. White has king e3, rook g8 and bishop c7. Black’s pawns advance toward the first rank. After d2 and Rd8, the rook stops promotion on d1 down the d-file, while the bishop prevents the h-pawn from advancing to h2 along c7–d6–e5–f4–g3–h2. These duties are independent until you notice that their lines cross on d6. A sacrifice on that crossing can make one defender obstruct the other.'''),
        section('Occupy the crossing with a bishop', '''Bd6 places Black’s b4 bishop where either white defender can capture it. If Rxd6, White’s rook stands on the c7 bishop’s diagonal. Black then advances h2. The rook can capture the d2 pawn, but it cannot simultaneously remove itself from the bishop’s path in time to stop h1 promotion. If Bxd6 instead, White’s bishop occupies the d-file between the rook and d1. Black immediately promotes the d-pawn. In both branches the capturing defender becomes an obstacle, although the pawn that succeeds is different.'''),
        section('Check the forcing details rather than the picture', '''Before advancing the sacrifice, trace both defensive paths square by square and predict which pawn promotes after each capture. Then play both replays. The first includes Rxd2, a practical attempt to stop one pawn after the interference, followed by h1=Q. The second ends with d1=Q. White is not legally obliged to capture Bd6, so the two acceptance lines explain the mechanism rather than listing all possible resistance. The initial pawn advance and the black bishops’ checking resources prevent the simple alternatives from being an automatic solution.'''),
        section('Interference belongs to a family of ideas', '''Ordinary obstruction closes an escape square or a line; interference uses the crossing of two lines so either capture causes a different obstruction. Similar geometry can arise between a rook and bishop near a king or between defenders of two promotion squares. First identify what each enemy piece must defend. Only then look for a square that disrupts both jobs. An attractive crossing square has no tactical value if the opponent can ignore the offer and meet both threats. Always test quiet refusals, checks against your own king, and captures of the advancing pawn.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 303: the rook capture blocks the bishop',P['INTERFERENCE'],('d2','Rd8','Bd6','Rxd6','h2','Rxd2','h1=Q'),(
        'Black’s d- and h-pawns are near promotion. White’s rook and bishop can stop different pawns.',
        'd2 creates the immediate threat of promotion on d1.',
        'Rd8 stops the d-pawn. Its defensive file crosses the c7 bishop’s diagonal to h2 on d6.',
        'Bd6 occupies that crossing. Calculate both possible captures before advancing.',
        'Rxd6 removes the offered bishop but blocks White’s own c7 bishop from seeing h1.',
        'h2 threatens h1=Q. The c7 bishop’s diagonal is still obstructed.',
        'Rxd2 removes the other pawn, but one rook cannot stop both routes in time.',
        'h1=Q completes the h-pawn’s promotion because the interference consumed White’s defensive tempo.',
    )),example('The bishop capture blocks the rook',P['INTERFERENCE'],('d2','Rd8','Bd6','Bxd6','d1=Q'),(
        'Try the other capture of the bishop on d6 and identify the newly blocked line.',
        'd2 again brings the pawn one step from promotion.',
        'Rd8 guards the d1 square from behind.',
        'Bd6 offers the same interference sacrifice.',
        'Bxd6 places White’s own bishop between the d8 rook and d1.',
        'd1=Q promotes because the rook cannot pass through its bishop.',
    ))),
    ('A defender can block a friendly piece after making a capture.', 'A diagram of two capture branches is not an exhaustive proof of every refusal.'),
    (check('ch5-interference-cross','Where do the two white defensive lines cross?',('d6',True,'The d-file and the c7–h2 diagonal both pass through d6.'),('h1',False,'The rook on d8 does not defend h1 along a straight line.')),
     check('ch5-interference-bishop','Which pawn promotes after Bxd6?',('The d-pawn',True,'The bishop on d6 blocks its own rook’s d-file.'),('Only the h-pawn',False,'The bishop has moved off its h2 diagonal, but the immediate demonstrated promotion is d1.'))),
    ('Assign each defender its job.', 'Find their crossing.', 'Calculate both captures and the relevant pawn.')),

'ch5-simplification': guide((174,175),
    ('Judge a combination by its purpose.', 'Separate mating branches from an exchange sequence.'),
    (
        section('A spectacular move can serve a modest aim', '''Black’s king is on g8, queen c3, rooks f8 and h3, knight d4, and pawns a7, b7, e6, g7 and h7. White has king g1, queen g5, rooks c5 and f1, and pawns a2, c2, f2, g2 and h2. Black has an extra knight, but both the queen and the h3 rook are attacked. A successful combination need not add another huge material gain. Preserving the extra piece while exchanging the attacking queens can already leave a winning kind of advantage.'''),
        section('Calculate each capture on g3 separately', '''Qg3 offers the queen on a square where three white units can capture it. These replies cannot be treated as equivalent. After hxg3, Ne2 is mate: moving h2 has opened the h-file for the h3 rook, and the knight’s check closes the remaining net. After fxg3, Ne2+ drives the king to h1 and Rxf1 is mate. Moving the f-pawn has opened the f-file for the f8 rook. Each capture opens a different line; identify that line before expecting the same finish.'''),
        section('The queen capture simplifies', '''After Qxg3, Black uses Ne2+ to fork the king and queen. The king moves, Nxg3+ recovers White’s queen with another checking tempo, and the knight then captures the rook on f1. Both queens and one white rook disappear. Black still has the two rooks and knight against one rook, with fewer attacking resources to worry about. The replay chooses legal king moves illustrating that sequence. Count the initial extra knight as part of the final balance, rather than claiming the queen offer has conjured every surviving piece from nothing.'''),
        section('Know when exchange is the payoff', '''Pause before playing Qg3 and state your purpose in one sentence: meet the attacks, keep the material advantage, and reduce danger. Now compare the three captures and explain why each helps Black. In your games, an exchange combination can remove an active enemy piece, neutralize a mating attack or enter a favorable endgame. It need not end in checkmate to be successful. Equally, do not exchange automatically when ahead: verify the resulting pawn ending, remaining king safety and whether your opponent obtains a fortress or perpetual check. Simplification is a concrete calculation, not a universal command.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 286: the queen capture leads to exchanges',P['SIMPLIFY'],('Qg3','Qxg3','Ne2+','Kh1','Nxg3+','Kg1','Nxf1'),(
        'Black is a knight ahead but queen and h3 rook are attacked. Calculate all three captures on g3.',
        'Qg3 offers the queen while preparing a knight fork and two different mating patterns.',
        'Qxg3 accepts with the queen and leaves both white king pawns on their original files.',
        'Ne2+ forks the king and the queen on g3.',
        'Kh1 is a legal answer to the check; the queen remains attacked.',
        'Nxg3+ recovers the queen and checks again, while attacking f1.',
        'Kg1 answers the knight check and illustrates the exchange sequence.',
        'Nxf1 takes the rook. Black has simplified while retaining a large material advantage.',
    )),example('The h-pawn capture opens the h-file',P['SIMPLIFY'],('Qg3','hxg3','Ne2#'),(
        'Keep track of the h3 rook and the pawn shielding White’s h-file.',
        'Qg3 again offers the queen on the critical capture square.',
        'hxg3 opens the h-file, exposing squares around the white king.',
        'Ne2# combines knight check with the rook’s control of the h-file.',
    )),example('The f-pawn capture opens the f-file',P['SIMPLIFY'],('Qg3','fxg3','Ne2+','Kh1','Rxf1#'),(
        'This time inspect the f8 rook’s route toward f1.',
        'Qg3 invites a different pawn capture with different consequences.',
        'fxg3 vacates f2 and opens the f-file.',
        'Ne2+ gives the forcing knight check.',
        'Kh1 is forced in this branch.',
        'Rxf1# invades on the opened file and finishes the mating net.',
    ))),
    ('Different capturing pawns open different lines.', 'A combination may preserve an advantage rather than create a new one.'),
    (check('ch5-simplify-purpose','What is the queen-capture branch primarily doing?',('Exchanging dangerous pieces while preserving Black’s advantage',True,'The final balance is favorable without requiring an immediate mate.'),('Drawing by perpetual check',False,'Black’s checks win queen and rook rather than repeat forever.')),
     check('ch5-simplify-files','What does fxg3 change?',('It opens the f-file for the f8 rook',True,'The f2 pawn no longer blocks the rook’s route.'),('It closes the h-file',False,'That capture moves the f-pawn and its crucial effect is on the f-file.'))),
    ('Name the objective.', 'Split distinct captures into separate branches.', 'Count the final material and safety.')),
'ch5-positional': guide((182,183),
    ('Recognize compensation based on activity and weaknesses.', 'Study the strongest defence before trusting a game result.'),
    (
        section('The material offer has a positional purpose', '''Black’s king stands on g8, queen c5, rooks c8 and d8, bishops b7 and e7, and knights f6 and g4. White has king g1, queen e2, rooks d1 and g3, bishops b3 and g5, and knights b1 and d4. White’s e3 pawn and d4 knight lie on the c5–g1 diagonal leading to its king. The b1 knight is undeveloped, and the advanced rook on g3 cannot solve every central problem. Black’s intended compensation comes from exploiting these relationships, not from an immediate forced checkmate.'''),
        section('A second piece enters with a pin', '''Nxe3 offers the g4 knight to White’s queen. After Qxe3, Be4 attacks the undeveloped b1 knight along e4–d3–c2–b1. Meanwhile the queen on c5 attacks d4: the knight there is relatively pinned to the queen on e3. Moving that knight can expose the queen, but is legal. The d4 knight still blocks the c5 queen’s line, so White’s queen can legally move off e3. Calling that queen absolutely pinned would be incorrect: assess the obstructions in their current order. The d8 rook adds pressure down the d-file. In the game White played Nc3, allowing bxc3, then bxc3 and Bg6. White has returned a knight, and the new c3 pawn becomes a lasting target. The later pawn win requires further play.'''),
        section('Test another capture before retreating', '''When the opponent sacrifices a piece without forcing checks, investigate whether you can capture a second piece. Here Bxf6 is the important defensive try after Be4. Black replies Bxf6; Qxe4 captures the other bishop, but Rxd4 opens a sequence of exchanges that leads to an ending. Black obtains extra pawns and active pieces, yet White keeps practical chances with opposite-colored bishops. The source explicitly distinguishes that defence from the easier game continuation. A sound positional sacrifice can produce an advantage that still requires substantial work.'''),
        section('Use concrete assets rather than vague activity', '''Pause after Be4 and list the actual targets, lines and restrictions. Saying “Black has compensation” is incomplete unless you can show what prevents White from consolidating the extra piece. Compare the main replay with the second capture idea before making a judgment. In your own games, consider development, king exposure, pawn weaknesses and entry squares when material changes. Then examine how long the activity lasts and whether exchanging an attacker removes it. A historical win is evidence of what happened in one game; it does not prove every opponent reply loses by the same route.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 304: returning a piece creates a weak pawn',P['POSITIONAL'],('Nxe3','Qxe3','Be4','Nc3','bxc3','bxc3','Bg6'),(
        'Black’s activity faces White’s undeveloped b1 knight, exposed rook and central pieces. Identify the queen’s diagonal to g1.',
        'Nxe3 offers the g4 knight while removing White’s central pawn.',
        'Qxe3 accepts. Black must now justify the temporary material deficit with concrete activity.',
        'Be4 attacks b1 while the queen and rook pressure d4. Before moving White’s knight, inspect Bxf6 as another defence.',
        'Nc3 is the game’s choice, returning material rather than solving every tactical problem.',
        'bxc3 takes the newly developed knight with the b4 pawn.',
        'bxc3 recaptures and leaves White with a c3 pawn that can be attacked later.',
        'Bg6 withdraws the active bishop. The tactical phase has produced a lasting pawn weakness.',
    )),example('A stronger defence forces an ending',after(('Nxe3','Qxe3','Be4'),P['POSITIONAL']),('Bxf6','Bxf6','Qxe4','Rxd4','Rxd4','Qxd4+','Qxd4','Bxd4+','Kf1','Rc1+','Ke2','Rxb1'),(
        'White is not forced to choose Nc3. Test the second capture Bxf6.',
        'Bxf6 removes the f6 knight before Black can consolidate the attack.',
        'Bxf6 recaptures with the e7 bishop.',
        'Qxe4 captures Black’s other bishop, but opens the d-file exchanges.',
        'Rxd4 takes White’s central knight with the d8 rook.',
        'Rxd4 exchanges White’s d1 rook for that rook.',
        'Qxd4+ recaptures with check, forcing White to answer.',
        'Qxd4 exchanges the white queen for Black’s queen.',
        'Bxd4+ recaptures and again checks the king.',
        'Kf1 is the source’s king reply.',
        'Rc1+ brings Black’s remaining rook down the open c-file with check.',
        'Ke2 answers the check but cannot protect the knight on b1.',
        'Rxb1 wins the undeveloped knight. Black has extra pawns, with an opposite-colored bishop ending still to play.',
    ))),
    ('Activity needs concrete targets and restrictions.', 'Do not mistake the game continuation for the strongest defence.'),
    (check('ch5-positional-aim','What lasting asset arises in the game line?',('A weak white pawn on c3',True,'The b-pawn recapture leaves a target after the tactical exchanges.'),('A forced mate on the next move',False,'Black’s benefit is positional and requires further conversion.')),
     check('ch5-positional-defence','Why examine Bxf6 before retreating?',('A second capture can force the sacrificer into a calculable line',True,'Taking another piece tests the concrete basis of the compensation.'),('A sacrificed piece must always be declined',False,'The right decision follows calculation, not a blanket rule.'))),
    ('Identify exact compensation.', 'Examine the second capture.', 'Assess the ending after the tactics.')),

'ch5-traps': guide((183,184),
    ('Distinguish bait from a forced combination.', 'Check what happens if the opponent declines the offer.'),
    (
        section('A tempting reply is still a choice', '''A trap offers an apparently favorable move that fails to a concealed answer. The opponent is free to avoid the bait. A forced combination earns its result against the relevant defences; a trap may work only against a particular mistake. The chapter’s examples emphasize this difference. Here an independent illustration uses the familiar Légal mating pattern rather than reproducing the book’s Steiner–Balogh position. White has developed knights to c3 and f3 and bishop to c4. Black has knight c6, bishop g4 and a king still on e8; most other pieces remain near home.'''),
        section('Understand the attractive mating branch', '''Nxe5 moves the f3 knight despite the bishop’s apparent pin to White’s queen on d1. If Black greedily plays Bxd1, White has Bxf7+, forcing Ke7, followed by Nd5 mate. The surviving knight from c3 supplies the last check, supported by the knight on e5. This is a legal and instructive mating sequence. Before playing it, identify which knight moves on each turn and why the king cannot take the bishop on f7. White’s queen loss is justified in that branch by immediate checkmate.'''),
        section('Now let the opponent defend intelligently', '''Black can instead answer Nxe5 by Nxe5 with the c6 knight. This removes the knight supporting the mating net and leaves White’s queen on the board. White’s opening trick has not forced a queen capture or a mate. The second replay deliberately ends after the refusal so you can compare the remaining material and attacking pieces. This exact position should not be confused with other Légal variations in which a preparatory move changes the defender’s options. Pattern names never replace checking the actual squares.'''),
        section('Build traps into useful moves', '''Before advancing the offer, calculate both acceptance and the strongest refusal. Ask whether your first move still develops a piece, answers a threat or improves the position if the opponent sees everything. If it only wastes time and hopes for greed, it is usually a poor practical choice. When defending, take the same attitude: a hanging queen may be bait, but refusing every offer out of fear is also a mistake. Look for forcing replies, count the exchange, then compare a safe alternative. The entertainment pages repeatedly reward understanding the opponent’s intention before pursuing your own attractive plan.'''),
    ),
    (example('Independent Légal illustration: accepting the bait',P['LEGAL'],('Nxe5','Bxd1','Bxf7+','Ke7','Nd5#'),(
        'White’s f3 knight appears pinned to the queen. Black also has a c6 knight able to capture e5.',
        'Nxe5 offers the queen to the g4 bishop. Calculate acceptance and refusal separately.',
        'Bxd1 is the tempting mistake. Black wins a queen but neglects the king.',
        'Bxf7+ checks with the bishop protected by the knight on e5.',
        'Ke7 is forced in this mating branch.',
        'Nd5# moves the c3 knight and finishes the net around the exposed king.',
    )),example('Independent Légal illustration: declining the bait',P['LEGAL'],('Nxe5','Nxe5'),(
        'Return to the same position and let Black challenge the attacking knight instead of the queen.',
        'Nxe5 again moves White’s f3 knight to the offered central square.',
        'Nxe5 captures that knight with Black’s c6 knight. White’s queen survives, but the advertised mate has been avoided.',
    ))),
    ('A beautiful acceptance line does not force an opponent to enter it.', 'Check the exact version of a familiar opening pattern.'),
    (check('ch5-trap-refusal','What disproves a claim that this queen offer forces mate?',('Black can play Nxe5 instead of Bxd1',True,'That legal refusal removes a key attacking knight.'),('A queen sacrifice can never be sound',False,'Many queen sacrifices force mate; this position requires checking the refusal.')),
     check('ch5-trap-plan','When is a practical trap most useful?',('When the first move also serves a sound purpose if seen',True,'Your position should remain satisfactory against informed defence.'),('Whenever the bait looks expensive',False,'The value of the bait does not make the first move good.'))),
    ('Calculate acceptance.', 'Calculate refusal.', 'Prefer useful moves over hope.')),

'ch5-move-order': guide((186,187),
    ('Prepare a threat by removing a tactical defect.', 'Compare the same moves in opposite orders.'),
    (
        section('Two attractive ideas are not interchangeable', '''White has king g1, queen h3, rooks b1 and f1, bishop f4, knight b5, and pawns a2, c4, f2, g2 and h2. Black has king c8, queen d7, rooks d8 and h8, bishop c5, knight f6, and several pawns including a7 and b7. White wants Qa3, bringing the queen toward a8 and the black king. But the knight on b5 is exposed to Black’s queen. Playing the queen move immediately lets Qxb5 remove an attacker and spoil the intended attack.'''),
        section('Use a forcing preparation', '''Nxa7+ captures the a7 pawn with check. In this exact position Bxa7 is the only legal answer. The knight has done its job: it has gained a tempo, removed a pawn and drawn the c5 bishop to a7. White can now play Qa3 without leaving that knight hanging on b5. The resulting mating threats make Black’s defence difficult. Notice that the preparatory move is not merely a clever sacrifice added for beauty; it repairs the specific problem that made the immediate queen move fail.'''),
        section('Follow the pawn defence carefully', '''The replay follows b6 after Qa3. White answers Rxb6, offering the rook to the a7 bishop. If Bxb6, Qa8 is mate: the bishop has been drawn away from the a7 square and White’s queen enters along the now-open a-file. Black can choose other legal moves, so this line is a demonstrated defensive branch rather than a declaration that every move shown is forced. The source also gives Bb8, when Qa8 wins decisively. Before the rook sacrifice, trace h3–g3–f3–e3–d3–c3–b3–a3 and then the a-file.'''),
        section('A practical move-order test', '''When a combination seems almost right, reverse its first two moves in your imagination. Ask what changes: a checking tempo, an escape square, an open file, a defender’s position, or the availability of an intermediate check. The chapter gives other examples where opening a file too soon allows the defender to block it. In your games, do not calculate as though you could play two moves in a row. Name the opponent’s strongest reply after the first move, even when the intended second move looks decisive. A plan becomes a combination only when its transitions survive that reply.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 310: check first, queen entry second',P['ORDER'],('Nxa7+','Bxa7','Qa3','b6','Rxb6','Bxb6','Qa8#'),(
        'White wants Qa3, but first inspect Black’s queen attack on the b5 knight.',
        'Nxa7+ removes the loose knight with check and captures a pawn.',
        'Bxa7 is forced. The black bishop is now on the diagonal that White can later exploit.',
        'Qa3 creates the intended threats without leaving a knight exposed on b5.',
        'b6 is the source’s sample attempt to meet the attack.',
        'Rxb6 offers a rook and threatens further queen entry. Identify the a-file before advancing.',
        'Bxb6 accepts the rook, moving the bishop off a7.',
        'Qa8# enters on the cleared a-file and checks the trapped king along the eighth rank.',
    )),example('The premature queen move loses the knight',P['ORDER'],('Qa3','Qxb5'),(
        'Play the two ideas in the wrong order and look for Black’s immediate capture.',
        'Qa3 aims at the king but leaves b5 undefended against the queen.',
        'Qxb5 removes the knight. The attractive threat did not repair White’s tactical defect.',
    ))),
    ('Check the opponent’s reply between your intended moves.', 'A forcing preparation must solve an actual problem.'),
    (check('ch5-order-defect','What is wrong with immediate Qa3?',('Black can capture the b5 knight with the queen',True,'The knight’s exposure is the move-order defect.'),('Queens cannot move horizontally',False,'Qa3 is legal; its tactical consequence is the problem.')),
     check('ch5-order-check','Why begin Nxa7+?',('Check forces a reply while repositioning the bishop',True,'The preparation changes the board before the queen enters.'),('It instantly checkmates Black',False,'Black can answer Bxa7, and White must continue.'))),
    ('Identify the premature move’s defect.', 'Repair it with a forcing preparation.', 'Test the reversed order.')),

'ch5-zwischenzug': guide((187,188),
    ('Question automatic recaptures.', 'Find intermediate moves that defend and threaten simultaneously.'),
    (
        section('An exchange does not prescribe the next move', '''White’s king is on f1, queen d1, rooks a1 and h1, bishops e2 and f4, and knights b1 and g1. Black’s king is on e8, queen d8, rooks a8 and h8, bishops b4 and c8, and knights b8 and f6. White plays Bxb8, capturing the b8 knight with the f4 bishop. A beginner may expect the a8 rook to recapture immediately. White is counting on exactly that response, because Qa4+ would then fork the king and the bishop on b4.'''),
        section('Name the intermediate move’s two jobs', '''Black instead plays Nd5 with the f6 knight. This defends b4 and threatens Ne3+, a checking fork against White’s king on f1 and queen on d1. The captured piece has not been recovered yet. Black uses the urgency of the new threat to earn time for that recovery. This is an intermediate move, often called a zwischenzug: an inserted action before the recapture everyone was expecting. It need not be check itself; a sufficiently serious threat can create the same practical obligation.'''),
        section('Compare the two continuations', '''In the game line White answers Kf2, moving the king away from the fork, and Black then plays Rxb8. The material exchange is completed after the useful knight move. In the comparison line immediate Rxb8 allows Qa4+ and, after a sample king response, Qxb4. White has exchanged the bishop for a knight and bishop rather than just a knight. Before advancing either line, identify what Qa4 checks and attacks, then explain why the defended b4 square changes that calculation after Nd5.'''),
        section('Include this check in every calculation', '''Whenever a capture occurs, pause before assuming the reply. Inspect checks, attacks on the queen, mating threats and moves that remove a second target. Do this for both players. The chapter’s other intermediate examples include a checking pawn advance that prevents a file from opening and a rook check that moves the rook away from danger before the king recaptures. In your games, the key habit is to update the whole board after each move. The opponent may prefer a stronger task to the one you intended to assign them. An immediate recapture is a candidate, not an obligation.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 314: defend and threaten before recapturing',P['INTERMEDIATE'],('Bxb8','Nd5','Kf2','Rxb8'),(
        'White can capture b8, hoping the rook recapture permits a checking queen fork on a4.',
        'Bxb8 captures the knight. Black should inspect checks and threats before choosing a recapture.',
        'Nd5 defends b4 and threatens Ne3+, forking king f1 and queen d1.',
        'Kf2 answers the threat in the game continuation. It is a defensive choice rather than a forced move.',
        'Rxb8 now recovers the bishop after Black has improved its knight with tempo.',
    )),example('Immediate recapture permits the fork',P['INTERMEDIATE'],('Bxb8','Rxb8','Qa4+','Bd7','Qxb4'),(
        'Compare the tempting immediate recapture with the intermediate defence.',
        'Bxb8 again captures the b8 knight.',
        'Rxb8 recaptures immediately, leaving b4 loose.',
        'Qa4+ checks along a4–b5–c6–d7–e8 and attacks the bishop on b4.',
        'Bd7 is a legal illustrative block; the bishop on b4 is still attacked.',
        'Qxb4 collects that bishop. Black missed the chance to protect it before recapturing.',
    ))),
    ('Recapturing immediately is not compulsory.', 'Intermediate moves may create threats without giving check.'),
    (check('ch5-between-roles','What does Nd5 accomplish?',('It defends b4 and threatens a checking fork on e3',True,'One move solves the loose-piece problem and creates a new threat.'),('It immediately takes back the bishop on b8',False,'The recapture is delayed until after White answers the threat.')),
     check('ch5-between-search','When should you search for an intermediate move?',('After a capture, before assuming an automatic recapture',True,'Checks and stronger threats can change the order of events.'),('Only when a queen has been sacrificed',False,'The same habit applies to ordinary captures and exchanges.'))),
    ('Do not recapture by reflex.', 'Find checks and serious threats.', 'Update the next move’s tactical conditions.')),
'ch5-endpoint': guide((184,189),
    ('Continue calculating after a material gain.', 'Count a knight’s legal escape squares at the endpoint.'),
    (
        section('A fork can succeed while its plan fails', '''Black has king h8, queen c6, knight c4, and pawns a7, b6, c5, g7 and h7. White has king g1, queen d1, bishop e5, and pawns a3, c3, f4, g2 and h2. Black notices Qxg2+: the queen seems to be offered, but Kxg2 permits Ne3+, forking the king and queen. After the king moves, Nxd1 wins White’s queen. Counting only those captures suggests Black has gained a pawn. The missing question is whether the knight can survive after reaching d1.'''),
        section('Calculate one quiet move farther', '''The replay follows Qxg2+ Kxg2 Ne3+ Kf3 Nxd1. White then plays c4. The d1 knight has only four potential destinations on the board: b2, c3, e3 and f2. Reconstruct those jumps rather than trusting a remembered picture. After c4, the bishop on e5 controls b2 and the king on f3 controls e3 and f2. The bishop’s diagonal to c3 has also opened because the pawn left c3. Every retreat is covered.'''),
        section('Why vacating a square can close it', '''The surprising feature is that c4 makes c3 empty, yet makes it unsafe for the knight. Before the pawn moves, the bishop’s e5–d4–c3 line ends at that friendly pawn. After it moves, the bishop controls c3. White’s king and bishop cooperate to trap the knight without checking it. The opponent still gets a move, so “trapped” means the knight lacks a safe retreat and can be captured later; it does not mean Black has no legal move at all. Black’s pawn moves do not restore the missing knight escapes.'''),
        section('Choose a stable stopping point', '''Before starting a sacrifice, predict the material balance and then inspect the final piece placement. Are any survivors trapped, pinned, overloaded or vulnerable to mate? Are there forcing checks for the other side? Is a pawn about to promote? The chapter also gives combinations spoiled by stalemate or by an overlooked defensive capture. These are all endpoint errors. In your games, add a fresh threat check after the expected payoff, especially when a knight lands on the back rank. A legal fork is only a local achievement; the quality of the whole sequence depends on the position it leaves behind.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 316: the pawn won is less valuable than the trapped knight',P['ENDPOINT'],('Qxg2+','Kxg2','Ne3+','Kf3','Nxd1','c4'),(
        'Black sees a queen offer followed by a knight fork. Calculate beyond the capture on d1.',
        'Qxg2+ captures a pawn and offers Black’s queen to the king.',
        'Kxg2 accepts, placing the king where Ne3 can check it.',
        'Ne3+ forks the king and White’s queen on d1.',
        'Kf3 answers the check and brings the king near the knight’s eventual retreats.',
        'Nxd1 recovers the queen. The material calculation is not yet finished.',
        'c4 opens the bishop’s control of c3. Together king and bishop cover b2, c3, e3 and f2, trapping the knight.',
    )),),
    ('Winning a pawn is not the same as improving the position.', 'An empty retreat square can still be controlled.'),
    (check('ch5-endpoint-c3','Why does c4 help trap the knight?',('It uncovers the e5 bishop’s control of c3',True,'The pawn’s departure opens a diagonal rather than giving the knight a safe square.'),('The c4 pawn directly attacks d1',False,'A white pawn on c4 attacks b5 and d5, not d1.')),
     check('ch5-endpoint-stop','Where should the calculation stop?',('After checking the opponent’s resources in the resulting position',True,'The last expected capture may leave a trapped piece or another tactical problem.'),('The moment the knight captures the queen',False,'White has the quiet reply c4 after that capture.'))),
    ('Count the exchange.', 'Check every retreat at the endpoint.', 'Look for quiet refutations as well as checks.')),

'ch5-choose-combination': guide((189,190),
    ('Compare candidate combinations by their final balance.', 'Avoid accepting the first familiar pattern you find.'),
    (
        section('Count before becoming attached to a pattern', '''White has king g1, queen h4, rooks d1 and e1, knight f6 and pawns b5, e3, f2, g2 and h2. Black has king g6, queen e7, rooks a3 and c8, bishop a7, knight f8 and pawns e6, f7 and h6. White is down a minor piece, although the exposed black king offers tactical chances. The familiar possibility Qxh6+ Kxh6 Ng8+ is easy to notice: the knight checks the king while attacking the e7 queen. Familiarity makes this a candidate worth calculating, not a conclusion.'''),
        section('Evaluate the familiar exchange honestly', '''After the queen sacrifice and knight fork, White can capture e7. Both queens disappear, White has captured the h6 pawn, and the material deficit of a minor piece remains. A pawn gain does not repair that deficit or prove the ending is favorable. The source uses this example to show how a player can find a combination and still choose badly. Before advancing the comparison replay, count the minor pieces remaining on each side and state precisely what the queen sacrifice has gained.'''),
        section('Look for a stronger forcing geometry', '''Qg4+ is the better idea. The replay follows Kxf6, which accepts the knight. White answers Qh4+, giving a skewer along h4–g5–f6–e7. The king on f6 stands between White’s queen and Black’s queen on e7. Once it moves, Qxe7 captures Black’s queen while White keeps its own. This is a different scale of gain from exchanging queens for a pawn. Kxf6 is a displayed acceptance branch; other legal replies to the initial check must be considered before describing an exhaustive forced result.'''),
        section('Compare outcomes, not spectacle', '''Pause before choosing Qg4+ and put the two stable endpoints side by side in your mind: queens exchanged with a minor-piece deficit, or Black’s queen captured while White’s queen survives. A more expensive-looking sacrifice need not be stronger, and a short move can outperform a famous tactical pattern. In your games, generate at least one serious alternative when the first line produces only a small gain from a bad position. Use checks, captures, exposed kings and aligned pieces to guide the search. The purpose of calculation is to choose well, not merely to prove that one attractive sequence is legal.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 317: the checking skewer wins the queen',P['COMPARE'],('Qg4+','Kxf6','Qh4+','Kg6','Qxe7'),(
        'White is a minor piece down. Compare the final material after the familiar fork and the queen skewer.',
        'Qg4+ checks the exposed king while leaving the f6 knight available to capture.',
        'Kxf6 accepts the knight. The king now stands on the h4–e7 diagonal.',
        'Qh4+ skewers king f6 and queen e7. Name the alignment before advancing.',
        'Kg6 is an illustrative legal king reply; the queen is still exposed behind the king’s former square.',
        'Qxe7 wins Black’s queen while White retains its own queen.',
    )),example('The familiar queen fork gains too little',P['COMPARE'],('Qxh6+','Kxh6','Ng8+','Kg6','Nxe7+'),(
        'Try the stereotyped queen sacrifice and count the final minor pieces.',
        'Qxh6+ captures a pawn and offers the queen.',
        'Kxh6 accepts and becomes a target for Ng8.',
        'Ng8+ forks the king and the e7 queen.',
        'Kg6 is a sample king escape from the check.',
        'Nxe7+ recovers the queen, but White remains a minor piece down despite winning the pawn.',
    ))),
    ('The first combination found need not be the best.', 'Include the material deficit that existed before the sacrifice.'),
    (check('ch5-compare-balance','Why is the familiar queen exchange inadequate?',('White gains a pawn but remains a minor piece down',True,'The existing deficit survives the exchange.'),('Knight forks cannot win queens',False,'The fork succeeds locally; its overall payoff is too small.')),
     check('ch5-compare-skewer','What changes after Kxf6 Qh4+?',('The king shields its queen on the same diagonal',True,'Moving the checked king exposes the queen behind it.'),('Black’s queen becomes pinned to a rook',False,'The demonstrated geometry is a skewer through the king.'))),
    ('Record the starting deficit.', 'Compare complete endpoints.', 'Keep searching when the first gain is insufficient.')),

'ch5-prerequisites': guide((190,192),
    ('Use weaknesses to direct the search.', 'Create a tactical configuration with a forcing threat.'),
    (
        section('Recognize signs rather than promises', '''Combinations tend to appear where active pieces face concrete weaknesses: an exposed or cramped king, loose pieces, limited retreat squares, or open files and diagonals. A local concentration of forces is often more relevant than the total number of pieces on the board. None of these signs guarantees a winning sacrifice. They tell you where calculation is likely to be useful. In this position White’s queen on g7, rook on f1 and knight on c3 face Black’s king on e7. White’s other rook is on d1, and Black’s queen is on b6.'''),
        section('Control the king’s escape before checking', """The first idea to examine is Qxf7+. Without preparation, Black’s king can escape to d6; the source gives a strong continuation with Nxd5, but it is a different construction. The pawn move c5 changes this exact detail: White’s c-pawn now controls d6. Consequently Qxf7 would be mate unless Black alters the position. A pawn’s attack can matter without capturing anything. Draw its two attack squares, b6 and d6, and connect d6 to the king’s escape route. That is the concrete reason for the preparatory move."""),
        section('Create the fork targets', '''After c5, White threatens Qxf7 mate. In the source’s main branch Black answers Qxc5, taking the pawn and occupying a square a knight on e4 will attack. Now Qxf7+ forces Kd6, and Ne4+ forks the king on d6 and queen on c5. The preparatory pawn offer creates one fork target while the checking queen capture creates the other. The source notes that immediate Qxf7+ is also strong with a different continuation. The exercise therefore asks for this demonstrated construction rather than declaring c5 the only winning move.'''),
        section('A useful search procedure', '''Before advancing c5, name the eventual fork square and both targets. Then ask what stops the immediate idea and which forcing move changes that condition. In your games, inspect the opponent’s weakest sector, compare the number and mobility of attackers and defenders, and look for a move that changes a critical alignment. When there is no concrete weakness or urgent threat, improving your pieces can be better than manufacturing a sacrifice. After every pawn move, run a fresh threat check for both sides: a single changed diagonal or retreat square can create a tactic that did not exist one move earlier.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 318: a pawn offer constructs a knight fork',P['PREPARE'],('c5','Qxc5','Qxf7+','Kd6','Ne4+'),(
        'White’s queen, rook and knight converge near Black’s king. Imagine a knight on e4 and identify its potential targets.',
        'c5 offers a pawn and creates the threat Qxf7 mate. The main branch attracts the queen onto c5.',
        'Qxc5 accepts. Black’s queen now occupies a square attacked by Ne4.',
        'Qxf7+ checks the king and removes the f7 pawn.',
        'Kd6 is forced in this branch, putting the king on the other fork target.',
        'Ne4+ forks king d6 and queen c5. The preparatory moves have created the required configuration.',
    )),),
    ('A tactical sign directs calculation but does not prove a win.', 'Do not label one illustrative construction the only winning move.'),
    (check('ch5-prereq-target','Why is the queen on c5 vulnerable?',('A knight on e4 attacks c5 while checking d6',True,'The pawn offer has placed the queen on a fork target.'),('Every queen in the center is lost',False,'Only the exact checking fork makes this queen vulnerable.')),
     check('ch5-prereq-search','What should trigger a tactical search?',('Concrete weaknesses and active pieces near them',True,'Loose pieces, exposed kings and restricted retreats suggest useful candidate moves.'),('A desire to sacrifice something every turn',False,'A sacrifice needs a calculable benefit supported by the position.'))),
    ('Locate a weakness.', 'Create the needed alignment.', 'Verify the forcing replies.')),

'ch5-recurring-patterns': guide((192,194),
    ('Remember relationships between pieces.', 'Verify the changed details when a familiar pattern recurs.'),
    (
        section('Store the mechanism rather than the coordinates', '''The chapter shows several combinations whose ideas recur years later in different positions. The useful memory is a relationship: one piece is deflected, a second piece enters with a mating threat, and the defender cannot perform both jobs. In Capablanca–Raubitschek, White has king h2, queen a6, rooks b7 and f5, and pawns a4, c6, g2 and h3. Black has king a8, queen d4, rooks b8 and e8, and pawns a7, b4, c7, d3, e3, g7 and h7. The a-file connects the queen and the rook that will move behind it.'''),
        section('Draw the queen onto the a-file', '''Rxa7+ captures the a7 pawn with check. Black’s queen answers Qxa7 in the source line. White has given a rook, but the queen is now on a7 in front of its king. The other white rook moves from f5 to a5, threatening Qxa7 mate. White’s queen on a6 currently blocks the rook, so Rxa7 is not legal. When the queen captures on a7, she vacates a6 and the rook on a5 protects her along the opened a-file. A quiet rook move has built a battery behind the queen and created an urgent mating threat.'''),
        section('The capture on a6 opens the finish', '''Black plays Qxa6, capturing White’s queen. This seems to remove a powerful attacker, yet the queen has moved onto the rook’s a-file and can be captured with Rxa6 mate. The rook on a6 then checks a8 through a7, while Black’s own rook on b8 and pawns restrict the king. This is the shown acceptance branch, not a claim that Black must take every offered piece. The tactical point is the pressure generated by Ra5, and the final capture makes that point especially clear.'''),
        section('Transfer without copying blindly', '''Pause before each rook offer and identify which enemy piece is being attracted or deflected. Then compare this position with a similar one: move the black king, remove a supporting pawn, or change the location of the second rook in your imagination. Does the mating net survive? The book’s related examples show diagonal pins becoming file pins when the king changes square. In your games, recall a pattern as a candidate generator and then recalculate it. Memory speeds recognition; checking the board keeps it accurate. Repetition is most valuable when you can explain both the shared idea and the important difference.'''),
    ),
    (example('Diagram 327a: two rook offers create an a-file mating net',P['PATTERN'],('Rxa7+','Qxa7','Ra5','Qxa6','Rxa6#'),(
        'White’s rooks can enter the a-file, supported by the a6 queen and c6 pawn. Black’s king is confined on a8.',
        'Rxa7+ removes the a7 pawn and offers the first rook.',
        'Qxa7 accepts. The queen is drawn onto the file in front of its king.',
        'Ra5 threatens Qxa7 mate: the queen would vacate a6, allowing the rook to protect her on a7. Rxa7 itself is blocked by the queen.',
        'Qxa6 takes White’s queen but places Black’s queen on the rook’s file.',
        'Rxa6# captures the queen and mates up the a-file. Black’s own units eliminate the remaining escapes.',
    )),),
    ('A remembered pattern still needs recalculation.', 'Check the actual king escapes and supporting pieces.'),
    (check('ch5-pattern-threat','What does Ra5 threaten?',('Qxa7 mate',True,'The queen leaves a6 to capture on a7, opening the a5 rook’s line so the rook protects her.'),('A draw by repetition',False,'White is creating an immediate mating threat.')),
     check('ch5-pattern-memory','What should you retain from this example?',('The attraction and support relationships, then verify the squares',True,'The mechanism can recur even when the coordinates differ.'),('An instruction to sacrifice every rook on a7',False,'Without support and confinement, the same sacrifice may simply lose.'))),
    ('Remember piece relationships.', 'Recalculate the changed board.', 'Explain why the final net works.')),
'ch5-king-hunt': guide((195,196),
    ('Calculate a long checking line in manageable stages.', 'Recognize discovered mate after an unusual king journey.'),
    (
        section('Inventory the attacking army', '''White’s king is on e1 with castling rights intact, queen h5, rooks a1 and h1, knights e4 and e5, and bishop d3. White’s other bishop has already been exchanged on f6. Black has king g8, queen e7, bishops b7 and f6, and several undeveloped pieces. White’s central knights, bishop and advancing kingside pawns can control squares around the king. The queen sacrifice on h7 is supported by a concrete sequence of checks, not simply by the number of nearby attackers.'''),
        section('Divide the journey into stages', '''Qxh7+ Kxh7 draws the king from shelter. Nxf6+ removes the bishop and drives the king to h6 in the main branch. Neg4+ brings the other knight into the attack and forces Kg5. Now h4+ pushes the king to f4 and g3+ sends it to f3. These pawn moves are not random advances: each gives check while taking away a square from the king. Before advancing each arrow, name the checking piece and predict the safe king squares. The line is long, but many decisions involve very few legal replies.'''),
        section('Bring the last attackers into place', """Be2+ drives the king to g2. Rh2+ sends it to g1, after which White can castle long with mate. Castling moves the king from e1 to c1 and the rook from a1 to d1. The rook on d1 checks along d1–e1–f1–g1: vacating e1 opens the line, while the rook’s relocation preserves it. The h2 rook covers the second rank and the g3 pawn controls f4 and h4. Before the final move, inspect the actual checking file or rank rather than assuming that the bishop must give the discovered check."""),
        section('Why length and difficulty differ', '''The final mating move also works with Kd2: vacating e1 uncovers the a1 rook’s line to g1. This example illustrates how a long forcing line can be easier than a short quiet decision. It also shows the danger of trusting memory for piece locations. In your games, split a king hunt into stages, update the board after every capture, and inspect alternative escapes. The replay follows the book’s famous main line; earlier alternatives are not all compulsory. In particular, White has other mating routes before the final position. What matters is explaining the checks and the final legal mating net accurately.'''),
    ),
    (example('Lasker–Thomas: a queen sacrifice starts the king hunt',P['HUNT'],('Qxh7+','Kxh7','Nxf6+','Kh6','Neg4+','Kg5','h4+','Kf4','g3+','Kf3','Be2+','Kg2','Rh2+','Kg1','O-O-O#'),(
        'White has two active knights and the d3 bishop. Black’s king is on g8; White can still castle long.',
        'Qxh7+ captures h7 and offers the queen, drawing the king out of shelter.',
        'Kxh7 accepts the queen. White must continue with forcing checks.',
        'Nxf6+ moves the e4 knight, captures the f6 bishop and checks h7.',
        'Kh6 follows the main line. Kh8 would instead allow Ng6 mate.',
        'Neg4+ brings the e5 knight into the hunt, checking the king on h6.',
        'Kg5 is forced, moving the king toward White’s pawn advances.',
        'h4+ checks with a pawn and removes g5 from the king’s shelter.',
        'Kf4 is forced in this position.',
        'g3+ checks the king with another pawn and controls f4.',
        'Kf3 is forced. The king has reached White’s half of the board.',
        'Be2+ checks along e2–f3 and drives the king toward g2.',
        'Kg2 is forced; the bishop and knights cover the alternatives.',
        'Rh2+ checks along the second rank. White’s h-pawn has cleared h2.',
        'Kg1 is forced. Stop and locate the a1 rook whose line White’s king is blocking.',
        'O-O-O# moves the a1 rook to d1 and vacates e1, opening the rook’s checking line to g1. The net is mate.',
    )),),
    ('Update captured pieces and bishop locations throughout the hunt.', 'A long main line may contain choices and shorter alternative mates.'),
    (check('ch5-hunt-stage','Why do h4+ and g3+ help the attack?',('They give check while taking away king squares',True,'Each advance forces the king onward through a controlled route.'),('Pawn checks can be ignored',False,'A pawn check must be answered just like any other check.')),
     check('ch5-hunt-length','Why can a long line still be manageable?',('Many checking moves leave few legal replies',True,'Count branches and forcing choices rather than move numbers alone.'),('Long variations never require checking alternatives',False,'You must still inspect every relevant defence.'))),
    ('Calculate in stages.', 'Update the piece inventory.', 'Verify who gives the final check.')),

'ch5-find-refutation': guide((209,210),
    ('Defend an urgent mating threat before seeking material.', 'Find a queen offer that both guards and counterattacks.'),
    (
        section('Do not resign before looking for forcing defence', '''White has king g1, queen d4, rook d3, bishop c3 and pawns a2, b2, c4, f2, g2 and h3. Black has king g8, queen e2, rooks a8 and f8, bishop b7, knight f6 and pawns a7, b6, c7, f7, g7 and h7. White has already sacrificed material to attack. Qxf6 takes the knight and threatens Qxg7 mate: the bishop on c3 protects g7 along its long diagonal. Black must meet that threat now. A large material lead is useless if the king is mated next move.'''),
        section('Defend the mating square with a queen offer', '''Qg4 places Black’s queen where it defends g7 along the g-file. It also offers itself to the pawn on h3. If hxg4, Black replies gxf6, capturing White’s queen. Both queens have disappeared, White’s attack has lost its strongest piece, and Black retains a major material advantage. The moved white pawn on g4 also interferes with the rook’s potential pressure along the g-file. This is a defensive combination: a seemingly exposed queen performs the essential guarding job and arranges a favorable answer to capture.'''),
        section('Compare ignoring the threat', '''The second replay deliberately follows the tempting Qxb2 instead. White immediately has Qxg7 mate, supported by the c3 bishop. There is no need to spend time taking additional material. This failure line makes the urgency visible and explains why a defensive move is necessary. After the sound Qg4, White can choose moves other than hxg4, but simply taking g7 would allow Qxg7. The queen’s new guarding duty has changed the mating net. Before advancing Qg4, trace g4–g5–g6–g7 and verify that the path is clear.'''),
        section('Search from the defender’s perspective', '''When a sacrifice is made against you, define the threat precisely before choosing a reply. Consider capturing the attacker, exchanging queens, adding a defender, giving a forcing check, or making a counter-threat that changes the attacker’s calculation. Here a nonchecking queen offer is stronger than a greedy pawn capture. In your own attacking games, actively look for that resource on the opponent’s behalf. The chapter’s refutation exercises train the same discipline from both sides: neither a spectacular sacrifice nor an intimidating threat excuses incomplete calculation. A defence can combine retreat prevention, guarding and material recovery in a single move.'''),
    ),
    (example('Exercise 35: the queen offer meets the mating threat',P['REFUTE'],('Qxf6','Qg4','hxg4','gxf6'),(
        'White has sacrificed material for an attack. Identify Qxg7 mate, supported by the c3 bishop.',
        'Qxf6 removes the knight and threatens the mating capture on g7.',
        'Qg4 defends g7 and offers the queen to the h3 pawn. Calculate the reply to hxg4 before accepting.',
        'hxg4 takes the offered queen, but White’s own queen remains attacked by g7.',
        'gxf6 captures White’s queen. The queens are exchanged and Black keeps a large material advantage.',
    )),example('Ignoring the mating threat loses immediately',P['REFUTE'],('Qxf6','Qxb2','Qxg7#'),(
        'Use the same attacking position to compare a greedy defence.',
        'Qxf6 again creates the supported threat Qxg7 mate.',
        'Qxb2 takes a pawn but leaves g7 undefended against the mating capture.',
        'Qxg7# ends the game. The c3 bishop protects the queen and Black has no legal escape.',
    ))),
    ('Meet the actual threat before counting extra material.', 'After accepting an offer, check whether your own queen can be captured.'),
    (check('ch5-refute-guard','What essential job does Qg4 perform?',('It guards g7 against Qxg7 mate',True,'The queen controls g7 along the clear g-file.'),('It gives immediate check to the white king',False,'The move is quiet; its force comes from defence and the counter-capture.')),
     check('ch5-refute-capture','What follows hxg4?',('gxf6 captures White’s queen',True,'The pawn capture removes the principal attacker after the queen exchange.'),('Black has no reply because its queen disappeared',False,'The g7 pawn can capture the white queen on f6.'))),
    ('State the threat exactly.', 'Find active defence.', 'Calculate both queens’ fate.')),

'ch5-counter-combination': guide((209,210),
    ('Answer a mating threat with forcing checks.', 'Connect attraction, discovered check and a back-rank finish.'),
    (
        section('Find the defender’s forcing moves', '''White has king h1, queen a3, rooks d1 and e1, knight d6 and pawns a2, b2 and h3. Black has king g8, queen f4, rook c2, bishop g7, and pawns a5, b6, f7, g5 and h6. Black threatens mate against White’s confined king, but White can check first. The active white rooks and knight make that check a serious resource. Do not begin by assuming the only choices are passive defence or resignation. Identify the back rank, the knight’s potential checking squares and the piece that can block the initial rook check.'''),
        section('Attract the king onto the opened line', '''Re8+ checks across the eighth rank. In the shown branch Bf8 blocks, then Rxf8+ offers the rook. Kxf8 accepts and draws Black’s king onto f8. The knight on d6 obstructs White’s queen on a3 along a3–b4–c5–d6–e7–f8. Nf5+ uncovers that queen check. This is discovered check: the knight on f5 does not itself attack f8, so it is not double check. Black may therefore block the diagonal as well as move the king. The source’s main line chooses Kg8, but four other replies must be checked.'''),
        section('The final queen offer has a different job', '''After Kg8, Qf8+ places White’s queen beside the king. Black can choose Kxf8, allowing Rd8 mate, or Kh7, allowing Qg7 mate. The knight on f5 protects g7 and h6, so the queen’s alternative finish is supported and the king cannot escape to h6. Notice how the sacrifices change purpose: the first rook helps establish the diagonal check, while the queen offer prepares two different mates. An offer is effective even when accepting it is not compulsory, provided refusing it also fails. Calculate both answers before calling the combination sound.'''),
        section('Counterattack requires a tree of answers', '''After Nf5+, Black’s alternatives to Kg8 are Ke8, Qd6, Qb4 and Rc5. Ke8 permits Ng7 mate; the knight checks e8 while White’s queen controls the remaining exits. Qb4 and Rc5 interpose on the queen’s diagonal, but neither stops Rd8 mate. Qd6 is met by Qxd6+: Black can then choose Ke8, allowing Ng7 mate, or Kg8, when Qf8+ reaches the two finishes already examined. The worked examples let you inspect these branches separately. The initial Re8+ also has other replies, so this lesson analyzes its Bf8 branch rather than claiming every initial defence was forced. Checks earn time; calculating the opponent’s choices makes that time useful.'''),
    ),
    (example('Exercise 34: forcing checks overturn the attack',P['COUNTER'],('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+','Kg8','Qf8+','Kxf8','Rd8#'),(
        'White faces a mating threat. Trace the queen’s a3–f8 diagonal and the rook route to d8.',
        'Re8+ checks across the eighth rank and makes Black defend first.',
        'Bf8 is the source’s blocking branch; other initial replies require separate calculation.',
        'Rxf8+ captures the blocker and offers the rook to attract the king.',
        'Kxf8 accepts. White’s d6 knight still blocks the a3 queen’s diagonal to f8.',
        'Nf5+ uncovers queen check along the diagonal. The knight does not check f8; Black may block or move the king.',
        'Kg8 is the main-line choice. Four other defences to the discovered check are explored below.',
        'Qf8+ offers the queen. Both Kxf8 and Kh7 are legal, and each allows a different mate.',
        'Kxf8 accepts the queen. Identify the remaining white rook before continuing.',
        'Rd8# checks across the eighth rank. The black king has no capture, block or escape.',
    )),example('Counter-combination: declining the final queen',after(('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+','Kg8'),P['COUNTER']),('Qf8+','Kh7','Qg7#'),(
        'Black has chosen g8 after the discovered check. Calculate both replies to the queen offer.',
        'Qf8+ checks along the eighth rank and offers the queen.',
        'Kh7 declines the queen. The knight f5 controls g7 and h6.',
        'Qg7# is protected by Nf5 and seals the king’s remaining squares.',
    )),example('Counter-combination: the king chooses e8',after(('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+'),P['COUNTER']),('Ke8','Ng7#'),(
        'Only the queen checks along a3–f8; Black can also choose the e8 square.',
        'Ke8 leaves f8 but enters the knight’s next checking pattern.',
        'Ng7# checks e8. The queen on a3 and knight together cover the escape squares.',
    )),example('Counter-combination: blocking with the queen on d6',after(('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+'),P['COUNTER']),('Qd6','Qxd6+','Kg8','Qf8+','Kxf8','Rd8#'),(
        'A discovered single check can be blocked. Black’s queen can reach d6.',
        'Qd6 interposes on a3–f8, but White can capture that queen.',
        'Qxd6+ removes the block with check along d6–e7–f8.',
        'Kg8 chooses the longer defence. The alternative Ke8 allows Ng7# immediately.',
        'Qf8+ repeats the queen offer. Kh7 would allow Qg7# instead.',
        'Kxf8 accepts and places the king on the rook’s final target rank.',
        'Rd8# ends this branch with the same back-rank pattern.',
    )),example('Counter-combination: blocking on b4',after(('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+'),P['COUNTER']),('Qb4','Rd8#'),(
        'Black may block the diagonal on b4 instead of moving the king.',
        'Qb4 stops the queen check, but leaves the eighth rank vulnerable.',
        'Rd8# checks f8; the black queen cannot reach a square that blocks or captures this rook.',
    )),example('Counter-combination: blocking with the rook',after(('Re8+','Bf8','Rxf8+','Kxf8','Nf5+'),P['COUNTER']),('Rc5','Rd8#'),(
        'Black’s rook can also interpose on c5, between the white queen and king.',
        'Rc5 stops the diagonal check without securing the eighth rank.',
        'Rd8# mates. The rook on c5 cannot answer a check along the eighth rank.',
    ))),
    ('A check must have a calculated continuation.', 'Do not confuse the two rooks when updating the board.'),
    (check('ch5-counter-double','Which piece checks after Nf5+?',('Only the queen on a3',True,'The knight leaves d6, clearing a3–f8; a knight on f5 does not attack f8.'),('Both the queen and the knight',False,'This is discovered single check, so interposing on the diagonal remains possible.')),
     check('ch5-counter-last','What does Qf8+ achieve after Kg8?',('It prepares mate after either king reply',True,'Kxf8 allows Rd8#; Kh7 allows Qg7#, supported by Nf5.'),('It forces Black to capture the queen',False,'Kh7 is also legal, so calculate that branch too.'))),
    ('Find forcing defence.', 'Track clearance and attraction.', 'Verify the final mating net.')),

'ch5-practice': guide((198,210),
    ('Read the opponent’s intention before advancing a plan.', 'Combine breakthrough calculation with a fresh threat check.'),
    (
        section('What the entertainment themes train', '''The chapter’s supplementary material includes master games, Alekhine combinations, traps, finding combinations, saving difficult positions, and refuting unsound attacks. They ask for different results but share one habit: identify what the position allows before deciding what you want. A player can overlook a defensive sacrifice while celebrating promotion, accept a false fork, or underestimate a passed pawn. The app’s examples select representative mechanisms. This lesson uses an original simplified pawn position to isolate breakthrough; it does not claim to reproduce all the original puzzles or their variations.'''),
        section('Inventory a two-way breakthrough', '''White has king a1 and pawns f5, g5 and h5. Black has king a8 and pawns f7, g7 and h7. Neither king can intervene quickly on the kingside. The central pawn push g6 offers a pawn and creates two possible outside routes. If fxg6, White offers h6; after gxh6, f6 has a clear f-file to promotion. If hxg6, White instead offers f6; after gxf6, h6 has a clear h-file. The second pawn offer is the essential move that clears the other outside pawn’s path.'''),
        section('Do not treat the defender’s replies as automatic', '''The replays show the two adjacent-pawn captures to explain the mechanism. Black can also decline an offer or move a king; those choices need separate calculation in a full winning proof. In the displayed lines the black king remains far away, so White’s freed pawn promotes before it can arrive. Watch the pawn direction: White advances toward rank eight, Black toward rank one. Before advancing the second offer, name which black pawn must move and which white pawn will then pass. Count the remaining moves to promotion rather than relying on the visual impression of a clear file.'''),
        section('Turn calculation into a practical routine', '''After every opponent move, ask what changed and what they threaten. Generate forcing candidates, calculate the strongest defence, and inspect the endpoint for mate, stalemate, trapped pieces or promotion. From a worse position, search for perpetual check, stalemate, a fortress or a counter-combination; from a better position, search for those resources on your opponent’s behalf. The Alekhine examples add the same lesson in more complex settings through quiet preparation, interference, clearance and piece coordination. Work through a few carefully, explain every move, and test alternatives. The goal is a reliable habit of checking intentions and outcomes, not a collection of spectacular first moves.'''),
    ),
    (example('Original breakthrough: the f-pawn captures',P['BREAK'],('g6','fxg6','h6','gxh6','f6','h5','f7','h4','f8=Q+'),(
        'The kings are distant. White’s three pawns can create an outside passed pawn by two successive offers.',
        'g6 offers the middle pawn and makes Black choose between the two outside capture routes.',
        'fxg6 accepts with the f7 pawn, clearing the f-file but leaving that pawn on g6.',
        'h6 offers the other outside pawn to divert g7 off the f-pawn’s route.',
        'gxh6 accepts. White’s f-pawn now has no black pawn ahead of it.',
        'f6 starts the clear run to promotion. Count its remaining moves.',
        'h5 is an illustrative pawn advance; Black’s king cannot reach the f-file in time.',
        'f7 brings White one step from promotion.',
        'h4 continues the sample defence but cannot stop f8.',
        'f8=Q+ promotes with check. The breakthrough has converted pawn geometry into a new queen.',
    )),example('Original breakthrough: the h-pawn captures',P['BREAK'],('g6','hxg6','f6','gxf6','h6','f5','h7','f4','h8=Q+'),(
        'Use the same starting position but let the other neighboring pawn capture.',
        'g6 again creates the two possible breakthrough routes.',
        'hxg6 vacates the h-file and places a black pawn on g6.',
        'f6 offers the f-pawn to divert Black’s g7 pawn.',
        'gxf6 accepts. White’s h-pawn is now passed.',
        'h6 begins the outside run on the opposite flank of the pawn group.',
        'f5 is an illustrative counteradvance, too slow to stop the h-pawn.',
        'h7 reaches the seventh rank with promotion next.',
        'f4 does not prevent White’s next move.',
        'h8=Q+ promotes. The two capture branches share a mechanism but use different outside pawns.',
    ))),
    ('Do not promote automatically without a stalemate check.', 'Name the opponent’s threat before following your own plan.'),
    (check('ch5-practice-second','After g6 fxg6, what is the purpose of h6?',('To draw g7 away so the f-pawn can run',True,'The second offer clears the outside route.'),('To give immediate check with the h-pawn',False,'The black king is on a8, far from h6’s attack squares.')),
     check('ch5-practice-defender','What belongs in the final threat check?',('The opponent’s checks, promotion threats and drawing resources',True,'A favorable line is incomplete until the opponent’s endpoint resources are examined.'),('Only the material just captured',False,'Mate, stalemate or a trapped piece can outweigh a material gain.'))),
    ('Understand the opposing intention.', 'Calculate both breakthrough routes.', 'Check the final position again.')),
}
